#ident	"@(#)gen++:d.pkg/RELEASE.notes	1.29"

gen1.4 9509:
* The rule checker now places its output in file.OO in the current
  directory -- not in the directory of the source file.
* Similarly, metrics.query puts output files iin the current directory.
* fix to bug "Expression not one of variants..." for exprs of the
  form ::new/::delete
* performance impprovements
* added README.nmake
* Added source file mknewname.c, used by the rule checker, metrics query
  and examples.  Contains some useful auxiliary functions.
* Updated generic makefile..  Note: It is important to update any makefiles
  you have derived from this file in previous releases.  This version of gen++
  builds analyzers somewhat differently.
  Corresponding changes have been made in rc/makefile.
* Changes: FunctionSpec||signature hasa been renamed sigstring.
  It contains a "mangled" representation of the function arg types.
  A new print_string 'signature' has also been added to FunctionSpec.
  It is similar to 'header' except that it prints only the return type,
  name and arg types of a function -- not the arg names, default args,
  containing class or linkage specification.

gen1.3 9503:
* genoa bug fixes
* fix to node type spec and documentation
* upgrade to version 3 of OO rules and guidelines (including update of
  rule checker).  The new rule checker is called Checker instead of RC,
  to match the paper.
* changes to directory structure and makefiles.  The gfront/ subdirectory
  has been eliminated as it is not used.  This is reflected in the makefiles.

gen1.2 9411:
* added a license manager
  To run either cplang or any of the analyzers built using cplang, it is
  necessary to export GENPP_LICENSE_FILE and set it to ggg/GENPP.dat,
  where ggg is the full path of the gen++ installation directory.
  See also the contents of QCC.
* added two hypertext-form documentation files: typesummary.html
  and typedocs.html. These files provide a Mosaic (or other WWW
  browser) style access to the information about the types, slots
  and fillers of the C++ parse tree used by GEN++. 
* added OO rule checker
  In addition ot the sample queries found in the examples directory,
  the current releasae also contians source for the OO rule checker.
  See the contents of directory rc/.
* added OO metrics suite.  In addition to the sample queries,
  the current release also contains source for the OO metrics
  suite developed a t MIT.  See the contents of metrics/.
* added support for C_File interface to stdio
  FILEs are now treated like GNODEs and can thus be passed
  as ARGs to functions and procedures.  Note that this means
  that FILE entities (like stdout, stderr, etc) can no longer
  be used directly in gen++ constructs.  For example,
	(PRINT stdout "...")
  is no longer legal.  To use stdout you must first assign to
  a gen++ variable, then use the variable:
	(ASSIGN STDOUT (CEXPR C_File "stdout"))
	...
	(PRINT STDOUT "...")
  NOTE: Do not confuse the node type C_File with the node type
  CFile.  The latter is the top node of a complete parse tree.
  The former is gen++'s interface to stdio FILE structures.
  NOTE ALSO: As with C_Integer, C_Float and C_String, it is necessary
  to convert the value of a GNODE variable of type C_File within
  C expressions.  A new macro getCfile, analogous to the other C
  conversion macros has been provided for this purpose.  File
  descriptors are still declared as "FILE fd".
  Sample queries have been updated to reflect the change.
* improved garbage collection
* front end bug fixes
* added FileSpec to spec + list of included files to CFile
  This allows the user to scan the list of included files.

gen1.1 9406:
* Scope_Private, Scope_Protected and Scope_Public have been mreged
  into a new Scope_Member.  This should simplify queries that test
  for various types of scopes
* NameRef has a new print_string called 'mangled', that prints the
  "mangled" or encoded id of a given c++ name.
  Use like any other print_string:
	(PRINT FD "%s" <mangled $token>)
* cfront front end upgrade to 3.0.3
* fix to handling of pointers to members

gen1.0 9403:
* The following genoa commands were added and are documented in the new manual:
  - OPEN, CLOSE (for file I/O)
  - PREV (analogous to NEXT on list elements)
  - TRAVERSE (previously an undocumented command called SEARCH)
    SEARCH is still accepted with the same syntax for compatibility
* a number of bugs have been fixed
* the manual has been updated

gen0.3 940120:
* fixes to queries
* fixes for internal errors
* updated manual
* fixes for handling of default arguments:
  - function declarations now contain arg Declarations (instead of NameRefs)
    so names referenced in default args are found in global searches.
    NOTE: Slots declarations for type, returntype and args changed slightly.
          In particular, FunctionSpec defines !type instead of inheriting it;
          returntype and args are also no longer suppressed ('!' removed).
          This should have no impact on existing queries, but should improve
          the coverage of global searches.
  - NIT: Default args accumulate, so default args appearing in one function
    declarration will be implicitly present in subsequent declarations.
    For example:
      int x = 5;
      void f(int, char* = "fizzle",int=::x); // explicit reference to x
      void f( int a, char* p, int i ) // implicit reference to x from last decl
      { ... }
    As with default arg substitutions in function calls, gen++ does not
    differentiate explicit args from those generated by the compiler.
* TRAVERSE construct (previously SEARCH, which was undocumented) traverses
  an arbitrary expression.  See the manual for syntax and example.
* other changes to spec:
  - FunctionLinkage is now displayed in unparsing output.
    Also, FunctionType||linkage has been moved to FunctionSpec||linkage.
  - MemberSpec now has a location
  - NameRef restructured slightly:
    o scope slot moved into NameRef since it applies to all variants
    o hierarchy now structured as follows:
        NameRef
          AccessedName
          LabelName
          TypedName  # NEW: all names with associated types
            IdName
            FunctionName
            TypeName
  - VisibilitySpec and AccessedName now contain a slot
      !enclosing_class
  - MemInit||init has been changed from an Expression to a list of Expression
* Print strings have been added to various node types to provide alternate
  ways of displaying the gtree.  Without print strings, gen++ uses the default
  unparsing algorithm for each node.
  Print strings are special slots with no fillers that specify an alrogithm
  for display the given node.
  Print can only be used in the PRINT construct in a query.
  Print strings supplied in this release include:
    NameRef||verbose
          # prints different verbose information for various types of names
          # For example, FunctionNames||verbose prints the function prototype
    FunctionSpec||header
          # prints the complete function prototype without arg names, defaults,
          #   initializers or function body
  The syntax for using print strings is illustrated in the following example,
  taken from gref.query in the package:
      (PRINT FD "%s: in %s: reference to %s\n" $location
                <fname verbose>   /* use print string 'verbose' for fname  */
                <$token verbose>  /* use print string 'verbose' for $token */
      )

gen0.2 931110 and gen0.2a 931230:
* new list operations
  (LIST-OF nodetype arg1 arg2 ...)
	returns a list of nodes of type nodetype containing elements arg1 ...
  (CAR list)
  (CDR list)
	like analogous LISP operations
* numerous improvements / fixes to unparsing
  -- i.e., output from (PRINT fd "%s" $token)
     is now closer to legal c++ and contains fewer destructive rewrites.
  However, code output from unparsing prints still isn't always complete
  or legal c++.
* changes to statements:
	Statement||successor -- the runtime successor of a statement.
	Break||enclosing     -- the nearest enclosing loop or switch
	Continue||enclosing  -- the nearest enclosing loop
  LabelStmt||successors ahs been removed.
  Also,
	Builtin||attrs
  has been moved to
	Type||t_attrs
  and new attributes
	TA_Const and TA_Volatile
  have been added to type attributes.
  Notes on const/volatile:
   - const member functions or function types are identified by
	FA_Const  -- for function declarations or
	FTA_Const -- for function types
     ex: int X::f()const;
         // FunctionSpec||attrs contains FA_Const
         // FunctionSpec||type||attrs contains FTA_Const
         p = (int(*)()const)0;
         // UserCast||type||attrs contains FTA_Const
   - const/volatile pointers are identified by the CV_Spec Const
     and/or Volatile (see PtrType||attrs and RefType||attrs).
     ex: char *const p;   // PtrType||attrs contains Const
         int &volatile q; // RefType||attrs contains Volatile
   - other const types:
	const int ci;   // Type||t_attrs contains TA_Const
	const X* p[10]; // array of pointers to const X objects
	                // ArrayType||element_type||points_to is a NamedType
	                //    with t_attrs containing TA_Const

gen0.1 930914:
* bug fixes
* add Scope_Label for label names
* change label / goto names from strings to NameRefs
* new documentation generation
* optimizations to gtree processing
* add OTHERLIBS to makefiles for def of external functions
* some unparsing fixes
* queries

pkg 930430:
* built-in node types String/Integer/Float are now C_String/C_Integer/C_Float
  to avoid name clashes with C++ headers in generated code
* NameRefs and global search:
  - Name is now merged into NameRef (see new type.summary)
    All slots of type Name are now NameRefs.
  - references to type names (as in AggrType, EnumType and TypeRef)
    now include a NameRef slot so searches for arbitrary NameRefs
    will now also locate explicit refs to type names.
  - Ccast||totype is now moved to descendent nodes and is
    marked 'noexpand' (!) in GeneratedCast and 'expand' in UserCast.
    This results in global search finding type name refs in explicit
    casts, but not compiler-generated casts.
    For example,
        X* p = (X*)q; // global search finds reference to X
        B* bp = new D;// type ref in implicit cast not found
* pointer to member:
  - slot 'member_of : NameRef' added to PtrType/RefType
  - .*/->* now accessible as P2MemberRef (see type.summary for type hierarchy)
  - turn off cfront rewrite for pointer to data member
    These are now treated similar to pointer to member function in the
    interface.
* misc fixes:
  - global searches can now start at lists; for example:
        <globals [ ... ]>
  - member function calls are now printed correctly
  - preserve more refs to type names (as in arg prototypes, etc)
  - avoid null type slots (use T_Any instead of null)
  - fix some internal errors
* update and improve sample queries

pkg930331:
* misc bug fixes
* FunctionAttr now applies to function declarations instead of types.
  FunctionTypeAttr is a subset of FunctionAttrs that apply to function types.
  FudgeAttr is removed.
  Attributes such as FA_Inline and FA_Instantiation, etc, now apply
  to function Declarations only.   Atrributes such as
  const, static and ellipsis are duplicated in both function type
  and declaration attrs. 

pkg930308:

* documentation
  - added file type.summary, summarizing the node type hierarchy
    and what each node type represents.
    To be used as a quick-reference guide in conjunction with specfile.
  - gen++.ps has been updated to reflect changes in the spec and
    sample queries.  Only the appendix and examples and parameterized type
    sections have changed.
    The appendix also contains more description of how to use specfile.

* for aid in debugging queries, GENOA now includes a statement
	(DUMP fileid expression indent)
  where indent is an integer representing the number units to indent
  the display.  Output is an indented prefix display of the gtree
  represented by 'expression'.

* fixed core dump on some Sun systems

* pointer to data member rewrite turned off

* changed handling of friend declarations
  Instead of making 'friend' an attribute of a declaration,
  MemberSpecs in class definitions now have the following hierarchy:
	MemberSpec:
	  AccessSpec     /* private:/protected:/public: */
	  VisibilitySpec /* Base::member; in derived class */
	  NestedDeclaration
	    FriendDeclaration
	      FriendClass
	      FriendFunction
	    MemberDeclaration
  This simplifies queries that iterate through member declarations
  by eliminating the need to test that the declaration is a friend.

* more connectivity between declarations and names
  Declaration||defname is the symbol table entry for a name.
  Name||def and NameRef||def point back to the definition or
  first declaration if undefined.

* unparsing is now complete and now uses some of cfront's print
  routines for pretty-printing.
  - printing Name or NameRef nodes now prints a fully qualified name.
    Queries should print Name / NameRef nodes directly instead of the
    'id' slot to get the fully qualified name.
  - expressions, statements and declarations are now fully unparsed.
    To avoid printing an entire declaration in a query, print slot defname:
	(?Declaration <defname (PRINT stdout "%s" $token)>)

* parameterized types no longer cause core dumps, and are now partially
  accessible.  Since templates are not typechecked, bodies of template
  definitions are not defined in the spec, since the untypechecked
  data structures in cfront have a different format, which could lead to
  unexpeced results.
  Formal and actual parameters are accessible, as well as bodies of
  instantiated templates.  See
	Template
	  DataTemplate
	  ClassTemplate
	  FunctionTemplate
	Parameter
	  TypeParameter
	  ValueParameter
	Actual
	  TypeActual
	  ExprActual
	/* Also see FunctionAttr and ClassAttr specs. */

* sample queries have been updated to use new spec


pkg930222: transitional (see below for full description):
	* core dump fixed
	* unparsing improved
	* friends updated
	* cross refs to declarations
	* doc / query updates

pkg930108: initial alpha
